{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789316034,"nonce":"vQuT/51iJmK+bR9ya6fngRmoOi8=","title":"Python: the pending-exception check after an inlined library call is redundant on the normal path","message":"Every fallible library call is followed by `py$exc != null`. Once the callee is inlined, its only raising path is a branch to a cold `zb_fatal`; on the other path nothing wrote `py$exc` since the previous check found it null, so the load and branch decide nothing. In the nbody inner loop that is two checks per iteration (`bodies[j]`, `math.sqrt`), next to the bounds check and the domain check that Python's semantics do require; the loop runs about 1.5x slower than the ZynML `@reference` port whose array index is unchecked.\n\nTwo ways out, neither small:\n\n- Load PRE at the join: the check's load has a known-null value along the normal edge (the previous check's false edge, with no store or impure call between) and a fresh value along the cold edge; a phi of the two lets the compare fold on the normal path. `load_cse` today drops every load at any call and forwards nothing across a merge.\n- Exceptions as control flow in the HIR: the cold path branches to the handler rather than setting a global for the caller to test. A different model from the pending global the frontend was built on.\n\nAlso seen: the loop's reload of the list length, which LICM no longer hoists because `zb_fatal` is an impure call in the loop body. A per-function summary of what a callee may write would let the length hoist again; `zb_fatal` reaches `Cls$__init__`, which stores through its `self` parameter, so the summary needs to know that pointer is fresh.","files":null}]}